        },
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12362430/comment/12471730",
          "id": "12471730",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=reschke",
            "name": "reschke",
            "key": "reschke",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677"
            },
            "displayName": "Julian Reschke",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Europe/Berlin"
          },
          "body": "Is this on the level of the JCR API?\n\nWhy would we want to decode entity references here? Entity references need to be considered when the query string is represented in XML, but here...?\n",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=reschke",
            "name": "reschke",
            "key": "reschke",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677"
            },
            "displayName": "Julian Reschke",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Europe/Berlin"
          },
          "created": "2007-02-09T15:50:07.260+0000",
          "updated": "2007-02-09T15:50:07.260+0000"
        },
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12362430/comment/12471758",
          "id": "12471758",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=mreutegg",
            "name": "mreutegg",
            "key": "mreutegg",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=mreutegg&avatarId=18658",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=mreutegg&avatarId=18658",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=mreutegg&avatarId=18658",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=mreutegg&avatarId=18658"
            },
            "displayName": "Marcel Reutegger",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Europe/Zurich"
          },
          "body": "Hmm, you are right. According to the XPath 2.0 syntax there are no entity references needed in string literals:\n\nhttp://www.w3.org/TR/xpath20/#prod-xpath-StringLiteral\n\nJackrabbit uses a XQuery parser because of the order by extension defined in JSR 170 and there you need to encode the ampersand.\n\nhttp://www.w3.org/TR/xquery/#doc-xquery-StringLiteral\n\nSeems like the XQuery is not really a super set of XPath, even though it claims to be an extension to XPath.",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=mreutegg",
            "name": "mreutegg",
            "key": "mreutegg",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=mreutegg&avatarId=18658",
